<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
	Reagent list for the taming store window.

	The list id is the FILE NAME. MultisellData.parseDocument reads it with
	Integer.parseInt(fileName.replaceAll(".xml", "")), so renaming this file
	renames the list and breaks the store button that opens it.

	THE -1 IN THE <npcs> BLOCK IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THIS FILE

	MultisellData.separateAndSend refuses to send a list to an ordinary player
	unless ListContainer.isNpcAllowed says otherwise:

	    if (!list.isNpcAllowed(-1) && (npc == null || !list.isNpcAllowed(npc.getId()))) {
	        if (player.isGM()) { player.sendMessage("... only gm are allowed ..."); }
	        else { LOGGER.warning(...); return; }          <- ordinary player stops here
	    }

	isNpcAllowed(-1) is the FIRST test, and -1 is a literal Set.contains(-1).
	That gives three states:

	    no <npcs> block at all   -> _npcsAllowed is null -> isNpcAllowed(-1) is
	                               false -> GM only, which is what this module used
	                               to do and why the window never opened for a player.
	    <npcs><npc>SOMEID</npc>  -> only that NPC instance may open it.
	    <npcs><npc>-1</npc>      -> isNpcAllowed(-1) is true, the whole block is
	                               skipped, and the list is sent to anybody.

	The GM branch does not stop the send either: it writes a message and then falls
	through. So "only gm are allowed" was never a permission, it was the only branch
	left after the NPC test failed.

	MultiSellChoose repeats the identical test at purchase time and skips its own
	npc / objectId / instance / 250-radius checks the same way, so -1 is what lets a
	purchase actually complete and not just the window open. It only works because
	PreparedListContainer copies the _npcsAllowed set by reference rather than
	rebuilding it; if that changed, the window would open and every purchase would
	fail instead, which is the confusing half-working shape to watch for.

	STATUS: the mechanism was confirmed by reading the core, and then confirmed on a
	running server. A player with GM status OFF opened list 9300 through the store
	page and bought from it, so "usable by an ordinary player" is no longer an
	inference. Keep the -1; it is what made that true. If a purchase is ever refused,
	this line is the first thing to check.

	There is deliberately no xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation on the root element. A
	relative "../xsd/multisell.xsd" resolves against the MODULE folder, where there
	is no xsd directory, so the parser logged
	"cvc-elt.1.a: Cannot find the declaration of element 'list'" on every boot. The
	attribute is only a hint and nothing here needs it, so it is gone.

	Why this is a multisell and not a merchant buy list: the BuyList packet carries
	only item id, price and a stock marker, with no display id, so the client draws a
	module item from its own tables, has never heard of id 9300, and renders a blank
	row. MultiSellList sends each product's display id, which is why the reagents
	show their real borrowed icons here and in the bag.

	PRICES ARE HELD IN TWO PLACES ON PURPOSE
	NormalTamingPrice and RaidTamingPrice in config/module.ini charge the store's
	own buttons; these two rows charge the window. TamingData.verifyMultisellPrices
	compares them at startup and logs a warning naming the row if they drift, so a
	price changed in one file and not the other is reported rather than quietly
	selling at whichever is cheaper.
-->
<list>
	<npcs>
		<!-- See above. Do not replace this with a real npc id and do not delete it. -->
		<npc>-1</npc>
	</npcs>
	<item>
		<!-- 10,000 Adena -> Broken Beast Flute -->
		<ingredient count="10000" id="57" />
		<production count="1" id="9300" />
	</item>
	<item>
		<!-- 250,000 Adena -> Ancient Beast Bugle -->
		<ingredient count="250000" id="57" />
		<production count="1" id="9301" />
	</item>
</list>